[[Aryna Sabalenka]] is the two-time defending champion. {{cite web|url=https://www.wtatennis.com/news/4108753/sabalenka-overcomes-pegula-in-us-open-final-wins-third-grand-slam-title|title=Sabalenka overcomes Pegula in US Open final, wins third Grand Slam title|date=8 September 2024|website=WTA Tennis}} 

Sabalenka, [[Elena Rybakina]], [[Jessica Pegula]] and [[Coco Gauff]] are in contention for the [[List of WTA number 1 ranked singles tennis players|WTA No. 1 singles ranking]]. Rybakina can attain the No. 1 ranking regardless of other results if she reaches the semifinals. Sabalenka can retain it, or Pegula and Gauff can attain it, only if they win the title and Rybakina fails to reach the semifinals. {{cite news |last1=McMillen |first1=Liam |title=How Elena Rybakina can become the world No 1 at the US Open without even playing |url=https://www.tennis365.com/tennis-news/elena-rybakina-can-become-the-world-no-1-us-open-without-even-playing|access-date=26 August 2026 |work=Tennis365|date=25 August 2026|language=en}} 

Aged 46, [[Venus Williams]] was the oldest woman to compete in the US Open singles main draw since [[Renée Richards]] in [[1981 US Open – Women's singles|1981]], after receiving a wildcard into the main draw. {{cite web|url= https://www.foxsports.com/articles/tennis/venus-williams-gets-a-us-open-singles-wild-card-at-46-with-maybe-a-spot-being-held-for-serena|title= Venus Williams gets a US Open singles wild card at 46, with maybe a spot being held for Serena|work=foxsports.com|date=18 August 2026}} Starting at 12:13 AM, the first round match between Williams and [[Sofia Kenin]] set the record for the latest start in tournament history, surpassing the previous record (12:08 AM) set by the fourth round match between Sabalenka and [[Ekaterina Alexandrova]] in [[2024 US Open – Women's singles|2024]].{{cn|date=August 2026}}

[[Kristina Liutova]] is the first female player born in the 2010s to compete in a major singles main draw.{{cn needed|date=August 2026}}

